<article class="env-lemma" id="0G2R">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G2R">Lemma <span data-tag="0G2R">28.5.12</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a Noetherian scheme. Let $T \subset S$ be a subset. Let $T_0 \subset T$ be the set of $t \in T$ such that there is no nontrivial specialization $t' \leadsto t$ with $t' \in T'$. Then (a) there are no specializations among the points of $T_0$, (b) every point of $T$ is a specialization of a point of $T_0$, and (c) the closures of $T$ and $T_0$ are the same. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Recall that $\dim (\mathcal{O}_{S, s}) < \infty $ for any $s \in S$, see Algebra, Proposition <a href="/tag/00KQ" data-tag="00KQ">10.60.9</a>. Let $t \in T$. If $t' \leadsto t$, then by dimension theory $\dim (\mathcal{O}_{S, t'}) \leq \dim (\mathcal{O}_{S, t})$ with equality if and only if $t' = t$. Thus if we pick $t' \leadsto t$ with $\dim (\mathcal{O}_{T, t'})$ minimal, then $t' \in T_0$. In other words, every $t \in T$ is the specialization of an element of $T_0$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>